      //
      // The truck's 'plastic' is a fine pebble read from arm's length across a cab. A panel is a
      // foot from your face and wears a WRINKLE finish: coarse, matt, and deliberately textured so
      // it kills reflections off the windscreen and does not show fingerprints. Same job, one step
      // coarser and with no mould seam — a dash is moulded in two halves and a panel is a flat
      // plate somebody sprayed, so a line down the middle of it would be inventing a joint.
      //
      // ⚠ EACH GRAIN IS A PAIR, NOT A DOT. A speckle of random light and dark pixels is NOISE; what
      // makes a surface read as bumpy is that every bump is lit on the same side and shadowed on
      // the other. So a light mark is laid down and its shadow goes one step along the light's own
      // direction — which is the whole difference between orange peel and television static.
      //
      // ⚠ AND THE SHADOW WRAPS. A pair whose second half falls off the edge of the tile leaves a
      // lit mark with nothing under it, and at the seam between two tiles that reads as a hairline
      // grid across the panel — the one artefact a repeating texture must never have.
