    // whose own note ("the generic gravel-and-vent tile") called it that. The deck is the biggest
    // single surface a building has and this is the whole of what it was made of.
    //
    // ⚠ SIXTEEN TEXELS COVER A WHOLE ROOF, which is the constraint that decides everything below.
    // `drawTexQuadPersp` maps this tile ONCE across the entire deck quad — there is no repeat — so
    // a texel is about a twentieth of a building and nothing here can be gravel-sized. What DOES
    // read at that scale is the thing a flat roof is actually built from: felt laid in parallel
    // ROLLS with a lapped seam between each, which is a handful of bands across the deck and is
    // legible from the air at any range the roof is drawn at all.
    // ⚠ AND THE ROLL COUNT IS FIXED WHILE THE LAP SCALES, the same bargain `SEAM_ROOF`'s ribs are
    // struck on and for the identical reason: a lap cannot be thinner than one texel, so choosing
    // the roll width as a realistic fraction puts half the tile under lap and the roof reads as
