// The Mode-7 camera: world tile-offset (dx,dy from the craft) + height wz → screen. When
// `chase` = { back, up } is given (external view), the camera physically sits `back` tiles
// BEHIND and `up` world-z ABOVE the craft — a real 3rd-person chase camera looking up the
// craft's own tail, so the whole world renders from behind the aircraft, not the cockpit.
export function makeCam(W, horizonY, depth0, v, chase) {
  const R = v.map ? (v.map.length - 1) / 2 : 0;
  // ── ⚠ A PER-SEAT FOV IS A FIELD OF VIEW, NOT A HORIZONTAL SQUASH ───────────
  //
  // `fovMul` multiplies the LATERAL focal length below, and until this line it multiplied nothing
  // else — so a seat that asked for a wider view got a wider view on one axis and the same one on
  // the other, which is not a field of view at all, it is an anamorphic stretch. The cab asks for
  // 1.22 when you are inside it and nothing when you are behind it (see cab-view.js), so the world
  // was 22% wider through the windscreen than over the bonnet, and that is the whole of "the aspect
  // changes when I get in the cockpit".
  //
  // Scaling the vertical focal length by the same number makes the two axes move together, which
  // is what a focal length IS. ⚠ AND IT CANCELS: the stretch is (W·fov·fovMul) over (2·depth·fovMul),
  // so a seat can pick any field of view it likes and still match every other seat's proportions.
  //
  // ⚠ `RENDER_TUNE.fov` IS DELIBERATELY NOT IN HERE. That one is a lateral squeeze by design — its
  // own note calls it a tighter tunnel — and both seats share it, so it cannot make them disagree.
  // Folding it in would change every frame in the game for a question nobody asked.
  //
  // ⚠ AND IT HAS TO BE `cam.depth`, WHICH IS WHY IT IS DONE HERE AND NOT AT THE CALL SITE. That one
  // number is the vertical scale for BOTH renderers — `cam.proj` reads it directly and the GL
  // matrix builds `2·depth/H` out of it — so scaling it once keeps the two in step by construction
  // rather than by two call sites remembering to agree. `gl:parity` is what would catch it if not.
  const depth = depth0 * (v.fovMul || 1);
  // ── ⚠ A SEAT IS NOT A TUNING CONSTANT ──────────────────────────────────────
  // `RENDER_TUNE.eh` and `.fov` are the GROUND camera for an aeroplane: an eye height picked so the
  // near foreground drops off the bottom of an unobstructed windscreen, and a focal length tuned
  // for a runway. The cab inherited both because it renders through this function with `height: 0`
  // — which was the right way to build it and the wrong place to leave it. A truck already has a
  // dash across the bottom third doing the job the raised eye was there for, so the lift only ever
  // reads as sitting too high; and at fov 0.82 the world is pulled toward the vanishing point, so
  // everything out of the screen is smaller than a driver three metres up would see it.
  // Two optional overrides, defaulted to exactly what was there before — an aircraft passes
  // neither and is bit-identical.
  const eh0 = v.eyeH != null ? v.eyeH : RENDER_TUNE.eh;
  const EHbase = Math.max(0.05, eh0 + (v.height || 0) * RENDER_TUNE.climbLift);   // additive + floor: altitude adds real eye-height so you climb above buildings; floor keeps the runway/ground from collapsing at eh→0
  const back = chase ? chase.back : 0;
  // ── ⚠ THE CAMERA IS NO LONGER OBLIGED TO SIT ON THE HEADING AXIS ────────────
  // `back` is a SCALAR down the craft's own heading, which is the entire vocabulary a chase camera
  // needs and none of the vocabulary a detached one does: there is no way to say "two tiles to the
  // left of the truck" in it, because left is not a direction this function had. `fx`/`fy` are a
  // WORLD-space offset added on top, and `ez` an absolute eye height that overrides the summed one
  // (a free camera's altitude is its own, not the craft's plus a chase lift).
  //
  // ⚠ AND THE DEFAULT IS ARITHMETIC IDENTITY, NOT MERELY EQUIVALENCE. Every existing view — every
  // chase, every cockpit, the helm, the deck-cam, the cold open — passes none of these, so the
  // expressions below reduce to `x - 0` and the old `EHbase + up`. That is bit-identical rather
  // than close, which is what makes this safe to put under nine callers with no pixel coverage. The
  // temptation was to solve it generally (resolve the camera to a world point and derive `back`
  // from it); that reintroduces `sin²+cos²`, which is not exactly 1, and every existing frame moves
  // by a hair. See scripts/shapes/freecam.mjs, which asserts the identity rather than trusting it.
  // ── ⚠ AND THE HEAD LEANS, WHICH IS AN EYE OFFSET LIKE ANY OTHER ────────────
  //
  // 'lookLean' is a free look from inside a vehicle, and leaning is a TRANSLATION: a pure rotation
  // swings everything by the same angle whatever its distance and reveals nothing, while moving the
  // eye sideways moves near things more than far ones. That is the only cue that says the cab you
  // are sitting in has volume. The world's share of it is a fraction of a pixel at this scale — the
  // interior is what visibly moves — and it is applied anyway, because a model that is only correct
  // where it cannot be seen is not a model.
  //
  // ⚠ HERE, NOT AS A SYNTHETIC 'chase' AT THE CALL SITE, and that is a fix rather than a preference.
  // A chase object is NOT optional-field-tolerant: 'EH' below reads 'chase.up' whenever chase is
  // truthy, so a stand-in carrying only fx/fy makes it 'EHbase + undefined' — NaN through every
  // projection in the frame. It draws a horizon, a sky and an empty green plain, which looks like
  // the map failing to load rather than like arithmetic, and none of the shape gates can see it
  // because they all build their cameras with chase null.
  //
  // ⚠ AND IT RESOLVES AGAINST 'ownHdg' WHEN THERE IS ONE. By the time this runs, 'heading' may be
  // the CAMERA's — a shoulder-check or an orbit has already been added to it — and "lean left" is
  // left of the TRUCK. Taking the camera's would swing the lean round as you looked about.
  const leanR = (v.lookLean && v.lookLean.x) ? -v.lookLean.x * CAB_LEAN_R : 0;
  const leanH = ((v.ownHdg != null ? v.ownHdg : v.heading) || 0) * Math.PI / 180;
  const fx = (chase && chase.fx || 0) + leanR * Math.cos(leanH);
  const fy = (chase && chase.fy || 0) + leanR * Math.sin(leanH);
  const EH = chase && chase.ez != null
    ? Math.max(0.05, chase.ez)
    : Math.max(0.05, EHbase + (chase ? chase.up : 0));   // floor the summed eye-height so a low vertical orbit never drops the camera below the terrain
  const hd = (v.heading || 0) * Math.PI / 180, sinh = Math.sin(hd), cosh = Math.cos(hd);
  const off = v.mapOffset, ox = off ? off.x : 0, oy = off ? off.y : 0;
  const cx = W / 2, FL = viewLatFocal(W, v);   // fov<1 compresses the world laterally into a tighter tunnel; fovMul is the per-seat override. ⚠ THE FLOOR READS THE SAME FUNCTION — see viewLatFocal
  // The free offset resolved onto the view axes ONCE, so the per-point projections below stay the
  // two multiply-adds they were. Both are exactly 0 when fx/fy are, which is what carries the
  // identity above through `projFL` as well as `proj`.
  const fFwd = fx * sinh - fy * cosh, fSide = fx * cosh + fy * sinh;
  // WHERE THE EYE ACTUALLY IS, in world tiles, as ONE derivation. Fourteen places downstream — the
  // depth sorts, the backface culls, the occlusion tests — each rebuilt this from `back` and the
  // heading, which was correct for as long as the camera could only ever sit on the heading axis.
  // It no longer can, and fourteen copies of an assumption is fourteen places to forget it.
  // Identical to what each of them computed when the free offset is unused: `a - (-b)` is `a + b`.
  const ex = -back * sinh + fx, ey = back * cosh + fy;
  // How much further from the camera a point is than it is from the CRAFT, along the view axis.
  // Every depth the painter queue sorts on is craft-relative and has to be shifted by this to
  // become camera-relative; it was spelled `cam.back` at each of those sites, which is the same
  // number right up until the camera leaves the heading axis. Exactly `back` when unused.
  const fwdOff = back - fFwd;
  // AND THE INVERSE OF projFL, ON THE GROUND PLANE. Anything drawn in the camera own
  // forward/lateral frame — the headlight pool is the one that matters — has no world position
  // of its own, and GLASS 2 needs one: the ground mesh is built at MAP-WINDOW tiles so that one
  // shifted camera can draw the road and the buildings standing on it from the same numbers.
  // Solving it here rather than at the call site is the same argument `rawF` makes three lines up
  // — a projection and its inverse belong to the same object, or they drift.
  // NOTE it is pitch-independent by construction: where a point IS does not depend on how the
  // camera is tilted, only where it lands, so this is the same solve under both closures.
  const worldFL = (aa, sd) => {
    const f = aa + back - fFwd, l = sd - fSide;
    const bx = f * sinh + l * cosh, by = -f * cosh + l * sinh;
    return [bx - back * sinh + fx + ox, by + back * cosh + fy + oy];
  };
  // The chase offset shifts everything `back` tiles forward of the camera (f += back); lateral
  // is unchanged. up raises the eye height (EH), tipping the nose of the view down onto the craft.
  // ── ⚠ PITCH: THE AXIS GLASS NEVER HAD ──────────────────────────────────────
  // Until now the optical axis was horizontal, always: `sy = horizonY + depth·(EH − wz)/f` is a
  // pinhole that cannot tilt, so raising the eye is the only way to look down and a plan view is
  // not expressible at all. Everything downstream leans on that — the horizon is a straight line at
  // a known y, the ground is filled below it, fog banding is per-depth — which is why this arrives
  // as an OPTION on the camera and not as a change to it.
  //
  // The maths is one rotation about the lateral axis, applied in view space before the divide:
  //
  //   u  = wz − EH                 height above the eye
  //   f' = f·cos θ − u·sin θ        θ > 0 tips the view DOWN
  //   u' = u·cos θ + f·sin θ
  //   sx = cx + (l / f')·FL        sy = horizonY − depth·u' / f'
  //
  // ⚠ AND THE DEFAULT IS A SEPARATE CLOSURE, NOT θ = 0 THROUGH THE SAME ONE. cos 0 is exactly 1
  // and sin 0 exactly 0, so the pitched form reduces algebraically — but `f·1 + u·0` is not the
  // same floating-point expression as `f`, and every view in the game (nine callers, no pixel
  // coverage) goes through here. Same rule the free-camera offsets were added under, same reason,
  // and scripts/shapes/freecam.mjs asserts it with Object.is rather than trusting it.
  //
  // The 6-DoF camera this opens up is currently used by the Modelshop preview alone. Before the
  // SIM flies with it, three things need answering, and none of them is in this function: the
  // horizon is drawn as a horizontal line, the sky and ground are filled as two rectangles split
  // at `horizonY`, and cloud/fog layers are placed against it. Under pitch the horizon is still a
  // straight line but it MOVES, and at a large enough tilt it leaves the canvas entirely.
  // ⚠ `camPitch`, NEVER `pitch`. A view object in this renderer ALREADY has a `pitch`: the
  // AIRCRAFT'S ATTITUDE, in DEGREES, which four other readers in this file divide by 26 or
  // multiply by π/180. Reading that field here as radians tips the camera by fifty-seven degrees
  // per degree of climb — the city hangs upside down and the runway swings round like a billboard,
  // which is exactly what the flight sim did the moment anybody rotated. `renderModelPreview` had
  // already named its own field `camPitch` for this precise reason and then handed it back to
  // makeCam as `pitch`, so the rename stopped one layer short of the place that mattered.
  const pitch = v.camPitch || 0;
  let proj, projFL, rawF;
  if (!pitch) {
    proj = (dx, dy, wz) => { const bx = dx + back * sinh - fx, by = dy - back * cosh - fy; const f = Math.max(0.06, bx * sinh - by * cosh), l = bx * cosh + by * sinh; return { sx: cx + (l / f) * FL, sy: horizonY + depth * (EH - wz) / f, f }; };
    // The SAME depth, unclamped. `proj` floors f at 0.06 so a projected point stays finite, which
    // is right for drawing and useless for clipping: a polygon straddling the eye needs to know how
    // far BEHIND the plane a corner is, and the clamp erases exactly that. Three call sites already
    // rebuild this expression by hand; the camera should be the one that owns it.
    rawF = (dxx, dyy) => (dxx + back * sinh - fx) * sinh - (dyy - back * cosh - fy) * cosh;
    projFL = (aa, s, wz) => { const f = Math.max(0.06, aa + back - fFwd); return { sx: cx + ((s - fSide) / f) * FL, sy: horizonY + depth * (EH - (wz || 0)) / f, f }; };
  } else {
    const cp = Math.cos(pitch), sp = Math.sin(pitch);
    proj = (dx, dy, wz) => {
      const bx = dx + back * sinh - fx, by = dy - back * cosh - fy;
      const f0 = bx * sinh - by * cosh, l = bx * cosh + by * sinh, u = wz - EH;
      const f = Math.max(0.06, f0 * cp - u * sp), uu = u * cp + f0 * sp;
      return { sx: cx + (l / f) * FL, sy: horizonY - depth * uu / f, f };
    };
    projFL = (aa, sd, wz) => {
      const f0 = aa + back - fFwd, u = (wz || 0) - EH;
      const f = Math.max(0.06, f0 * cp - u * sp), uu = u * cp + f0 * sp;
      return { sx: cx + ((sd - fSide) / f) * FL, sy: horizonY - depth * uu / f, f };
    };
    // Under pitch the depth carries a height term, so the clip plane has to as well.
    rawF = (dxx, dyy, wz) => ((dxx + back * sinh - fx) * sinh - (dyy - back * cosh - fy) * cosh) * cp - ((wz || 0) - EH) * sp;
  }
  // AND THE FULL INVERSE OF proj, WHICH IS WHAT LETS WORLD TEXT REACH THE GPU. Two dozen callers
  // build a sign’s quad, project its four corners and hand the SCREEN points to drawSurfaceText —
  // there is no world quad left by the time anything could use one. There does not need to be:
  // proj is invertible and every projected point already carries its own `f`.
  //   l  = (sx - cx) * f / FL          wz = EH - (sy - horizonY) * f / depth
  // and (f, l) go back to (dx, dy) by the same two lines worldFL uses.
  // ⚠ UNPITCHED ONLY, and it says so by answering null. Under pitch sy carries a height term
  // (uu = u·cp + f0·sp) that this does not undo, and a silently wrong answer would put a sign on
  // the wrong wall rather than fail. Only the Modelshop passes a camera pitch today.
  // ⚠ And f is CLAMPED to 0.06 by proj, so a point at or behind the eye cannot be recovered —
  // every caller already gates on f > 0.12, which is comfortably clear of it.
  // ⚠ AND IT TAKES A `pull`, WHICH IS THE WHOLE REASON THE SIGNS DRAW AT ALL. Paint on a wall is
  // COPLANAR with the wall. A painter’s queue puts it in front by sorting; a depth buffer compares,
  // and a tie is not a win — the sign z-fights its own building and loses, which reads as the
  // lettering simply not being there. Subtracting a little ground distance and keeping the SAME
  // screen point slides the corner along its own view ray toward the eye: `sx` and `sy` are both
  // ratios in f, so scaling l and (EH − wz) with it leaves the projection bit-for-bit where it was
  // and only the depth moves. Same job FACE_EPS does for a bolted-on part, done in the one place
  // that knows which ray the point is on.
  const unproj = pitch ? () => null : (p, pull) => {
    if (!p || !(p.f > 0.06)) return null;
    const f = Math.max(0.06, p.f - (pull || 0)), l = (p.sx - cx) * f / FL;
    const wz = EH - (p.sy - horizonY) * f / depth;
    const bx = f * sinh + l * cosh, by = -f * cosh + l * sinh;
    return [bx - back * sinh + fx, by + back * cosh + fy, wz];
  };

  // W/horizonY/depth ride along because they ARE the camera: this projection is a pinhole whose
  // focal lengths are FL (lateral) and depth (vertical) and whose principal point is (W/2, horizonY).
  // A second renderer cannot reproduce the picture without them, and deriving them again elsewhere
  // is how two cameras start disagreeing by a pixel that nobody can trace.
  return { R, sinh, cosh, ox, oy, proj, projFL, worldFL, unproj, rawF, EH, EHbase, back, FL, fx, fy, ex, ey, fwdOff, pitch, W, horizonY, depth };   // EH/EHbase/FL exposed so traffic, the own-ship and the volumetric clouds can be placed + sized relative to the world camera
}

// ── Depth-sorted face queue (painter's order without a z-buffer) ─────────────────────────────
// A 2D canvas has no depth buffer, so sub-parts of ONE building painted in code order would over-
// paint nearer geometry — a tower drawn after the terminal shows THROUGH it, a marquee shows through
// its own tower. While a sink is active (beginFaces), the building drawers don't paint immediately:
// each face is queued as { d: camera-depth, fn }, and flushFaces() paints them BACK→FRONT (largest
// `d` first). drawWorldObjects opens ONE shared sink for the whole tile loop, so a building's faces
// occlude each other AND their neighbours correctly; between-building order is the real per-face depth.
// Outside a sink (FACE_SINK null) every emitFace paints immediately — so the yacht, deck, HUD and any
// non-building caller are completely unaffected.
let FACE_SINK = null;
// ── ⚠ THERE IS NO "PAINT IT BEFORE THE BLIT" GROUND QUEUE, AND THERE CANNOT BE ──────────────
//
// A GROUND_SINK used to live here, drained immediately before the GL composite on the reasoning
// that the city would then cover what lay flat on the ground for free. That reasoning was sound
// while the GL canvas held the mass and nothing else. It stopped being true at GROUND_FULL: the
// GPU owns the whole ground plane now, the composite is OPAQUE across it, and a queue that drains
// before the blit is a queue that draws into pixels the next call overwrites. See the ⚠ over
// `emitGroundFace`, which is where its one client went instead.
//
// Ground SCATTER, collected as depth-tested billboards instead of painted. See gl/billboards.js
// for why a probe cannot do this job: a tree whose base is clear of a tower while its canopy
// leans across it is the common case, and the probe answers it "draw".
let SCATTER_SINK = null;
// The ROAD SURFACE, collected as flat world quads instead of filled on the canvas. Measured with
// scripts/shapes/armcost.mjs: with the mass on the GPU, 97% of the calls a frame still makes are
// path construction, and `stripeA` — the helper under every road surface, pavement, lane line and
// dash — is roughly twelve thousand of them. Buildings, by then, cost almost nothing.
let GROUND_MESH = null;
// THE SURFACE MOVES ONLY WHEN THE FLOOR DOES. With the 2-D raster underneath, the GL canvas is
// transparent over the ground, so a road left on the canvas shows through exactly as it always
// has; move it early and the kerb strokes drawn on top of it get blitted over instead.
let GROUND_FULL = false;
// ── THE OWN SHIP, AS GEOMETRY FOR THE GPU ───────────────────────────────────
// Open only while a GL frame is collecting. drawAircraftModel fills it with finished world-space
// polygons for the vehicle you are IN — never for a contact, which keeps it one object a frame.
// See gl/solids.js for what it is for and why it had to exist at all.
let OWNSHIP_SINK = null;
// ── THE DEPOT SHED, AS GEOMETRY ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// The shed's other sink. It is filled by drawVehicleBay with world-space polygons and drawn by
// gl/solids.js, the same depth-writing layer the rig goes through — see the ⚠ there for why the
// shed cannot simply join the GL mass, and the ⚠ at the bay's draw site for why it has to be
// collected INLINE rather than from inside an emitFace closure.
let BAY_SINK = null;
// ── AND THE ANIMALS, AS GEOMETRY ────────────────────────────────────────────
// The third client of gl/solids.js, and the one that arrived as a bug report. A goose was a baked
// CARD — a fixed viewpoint painted into a texture, hung off the depth of its own feet — so every
// pixel of its body claimed the distance of the ground it was standing on, and the floor at the
// rows the body covers is nearer than that. The depth buffer was right and the bird was buried up
// to its head. A card also snaps through six bearings on the ground and twelve in the air, blurs
// as it is magnified close up, and cannot be animated at all without minting a texture per frame.
// Handing over the triangles answers all four at once, and it is the SAME mesh the card was baked
// from — see faunaWorldFaces.
let FAUNA_SINK = null;
// ── WHY THE RIG IS, OR IS NOT, HIDDEN BY WHAT IS IN FRONT OF IT ─────────────
// Two separate mechanisms mask the own ship against the world — a coarse cell clip on the canvas
// (beginOcclusionClip) and a per-pixel one inside its own depth buffer (occluderWindow +
// maskRaster) — and when the rig draws through a shed anyway, NOTHING says which of them declined.
// Both fail by doing nothing, which is the same picture as not being wired at all. __glass2() reads
// this.
let LAST_SHIP_MASK = null;
let LAST_FLOOR = null;   // see lastFloorState()

// ── WHAT THE SEA IS DOING, FOR THE THINGS FLOATING ON IT ──────────────────────────────────────
//
// The swell is a pure function of a world point and the clock, so anything that needs to know how
// high the water is under it needs exactly four numbers — and it must be THESE four, the ones the
// floor shader and gl/water.js are being handed this frame. A hull that computed its own scroll or
// read its own clock would ride a sea nobody is drawing, which is the one failure mode here that
// looks completely fine in a screenshot and completely wrong in motion.
const SEA_NOW = { t: 0, roll: 0, amp: SEA_AMP, wind: 0, state: 0, foam: 1.2, kt: 0, hs: 0 };
// ── THE GLASS REMEMBERS THE DUNK ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// How far under the water the eye was on the last frame the floor ran. Written by 'drawMode7Floor'
// (which is where the camera's ground point, its floored eye height and the sea clock are all
// already resolved) and read by 'drawGlassInner', which paints the on-glass beads several passes
// later — the 'FLOOR_STATE' idiom, and for the same reason: two functions in one frame that have to
// agree, with nothing to gain by each asking the question again.
const SEA_DUNK = { under: 0 };
// How long the glass keeps running after a dunk, in seconds. Long enough that surfacing is an event
// you watch end rather than a frame that flickers; short enough that it is over before the next
// crest. The beads have their own lives on top of this, so the pane is usually clear first.
const DUNK_DRY_S = 6;

// ── HOW ROUGH IT IS, INTEGRATED ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// A reservoir, not a reading. See SEA_RISE_S in client/shared/sea-swell.js for why: weather is
// sampled per CELL, so flying across a squall boundary steps clear-to-storm in one frame, and a sea
// that went from glass to breaking in one frame reads as a rendering glitch rather than as weather.
//
// ⚠ AND IT SEEDS TO THE TARGET THE FIRST TIME IT IS ASKED, which is what makes a server copy
// unnecessary. `ground-accum.js` needs the server to integrate the snow because a player logging in
// ten minutes into a blizzard would otherwise stand on bare grass while it built; the sea's target
// is derivable from the weather that is already on the wire, so arriving in a gale simply starts in
// a gale and only a gale that ARRIVES has to build.
// ⚠ IT INTEGRATES KNOTS, NOT AN ABSTRACT 0..1. Everything downstream — the wave height, the whitecap
// coverage, the spindrift — is a published function of WIND SPEED, so carrying a normalised state
// and converting at each reader would be three chances to convert differently. One physical number.
let SEA_STATE = { kt: 0, at: 0, seeded: false };
function seaStateStep(v, now) {
  const forced = RENDER_TUNE.glSeaState;
  if (forced >= 0) { SEA_STATE.kt = clamp(forced, 0, 1) * SEA_FULL_KT; SEA_STATE.seeded = true; return SEA_STATE.kt; }
  const w = windFromView(v);
  // ⚠ THE WIND IS READ IN KNOTS, NOT THROUGH `fly`. `fly` is `clamp(kt / 18)` — the WINDSOCK's
  // curve, "0 hangs down the pole, 1 stands straight out" — which is right for a sock and saturates
  // at Beaufort 5. Read through it, every wind from a fresh breeze to a hurricane produced the same
  // full Force 10 sea, and the entire weather response was two Beaufort numbers wide.
  //
  // ⚠ AND THE STORM GRADE IS A FLOOR, NOT THE SOURCE. Sea state is fetch and duration; fog is
  // WX_HAZE 0.75 over a mirror, so driving this off the grade would put a gale under a fog bank.
  // But a squall that has not yet built a sea still has one somewhere, so it can only raise.
  // ⚠ AND THE 0.7 HAD TO GO, BECAUSE THE WIND TERM ABOVE IT IS DEAD AND NOTHING SAID SO.
  // The design here is that WIND drives the sea and the storm grade only ever raises a floor under
  // it — which is right, and is not what happens: the day's wind comes from the climate profile's
  // 'monthly_wind_kph', every authored profile sits between 10 and 22 kph, and 'windFromView'
  // converts at 0.54, so the live wind tops out at about TWELVE KNOTS. There is no storm term on it
  // anywhere in environment.js — a blizzard blows exactly as hard as a clear afternoon — so
  // 'Math.max' has always taken the floor, on every frame, in every weather.
  //
  // That made 0.7 the whole sea: a max storm asked for 31.5 kt and got a tallest crest of 1.93 m,
  // which is the "waves are weak in a storm" report exactly. At the full scale it is 2.82 m, and
  // the hydro's launch (section 21 of sea.mjs) was tuned against that same top of the scale.
  //
  // ⚠ IT IS STILL A FLOOR AND STILL A 'max'. The moment the wind becomes weather-aware this goes
  // back to being what it was written as, and a genuinely windy clear day can still outrun it.
  const target = Math.max(w.kt || 0, stormSeverity(v.weather, null) * SEA_FULL_KT);
  if (!SEA_STATE.seeded) { SEA_STATE = { kt: target, at: now, seeded: true }; return target; }
  const dt = Math.max(0, Math.min(30, (now - SEA_STATE.at) * 0.001));
  SEA_STATE.at = now;
  SEA_STATE.kt = stepSea(SEA_STATE.kt, target, dt);
  return SEA_STATE.kt;
}

// The surface under a world point, in the window frame (the same wx/wy the floor works in).
// Returns the height in tiles, or null when there is no swell at all.
//
// ⚠ IT ANSWERS FOR THE ROLL ALONE, exactly as the MESH displaces the roll alone. The chop is ~1
// tile — shorter than the Echelon's beam — so a hull fitted to it would be fitted to noise, and it
// would disagree with the geometry anybody can see, which is drawn from the roll.
//
// ⚠ THE AMPLITUDE AND THE CLOCK COME FROM THE TUNE AND FROM `now`, NOT FROM WHETHER THE FLOOR HAS
// DRAWN. The first cut took all four numbers out of SEA_NOW and therefore did nothing at all until
// `drawMode7Floor` had run and published them — which it does every real frame and does NOT do in
// any headless harness, so the hull measured dead flat with nothing to say why. Only the sea SCROLL
// is genuinely a property of the frame (the Helm chase streams the swell past a held position), and
// its absence is 0, which is the same sea.
// ⚠ ONE FORMULA FOR HOW BIG THE WAVES ARE, and both the shader and the hulls read it. Written out
// twice — once where FLOOR_STATE is built and once here — a retune moves the picture and leaves the
// boats riding the sea the game used to have, which nothing in a screenshot could show.
//
// The two curves differ on purpose: a swell is there in anything but a flat calm so it comes up
// early, and a steep short wind sea is what a BLOW makes, so it comes up late. One curve for both
// gives either a choppy calm or a gale with no short waves in it.
function seaAmps() {
  const forced = RENDER_TUNE.glSeaState;
  const kt = forced >= 0 ? clamp(forced, 0, 1) * SEA_FULL_KT : (SEA_STATE.seeded ? SEA_STATE.kt : 0);
  // ⚠ THE AMPLITUDES ARE DERIVED FROM THE WIND, NOT SET. JONSWAP for the height, split 60/40
  // between swell and wind sea — see seaAmpsFor. `glSeaGain` is the only taste in the chain and it
  // is named for what it is.
  const a = seaAmpsFor(kt, RENDER_TUNE.glSeaGain == null ? 1 : RENDER_TUNE.glSeaGain,
    (RENDER_TUNE.glSeaFetch || 50) * 1000);
  return {
    kt, hs: a.hs,
    state: clamp(kt / SEA_FULL_KT, 0, 1),
    // The threshold the shader tests `wv` against, inverted from Monahan's coverage law so the sea
    // has exactly the observed fraction of breaking water at this wind — see seaFoamThreshold.
    // ⚠ THE TEAR IS PART OF THE FIELD, SO IT IS PART OF THE INVERSION. The shader tests
    // `chop + tear`; quantile `chop` alone and the sea breaks over the wrong fraction of itself
    // the moment the tear is switched on, silently and in the direction nobody checks.
    foam: seaFoamThreshold(kt, seaTearGain()),
    roll: a.roll, wind: a.wind,
  };
}

// ── EVERY HULL IN THE WINDOW, AS A DISTURBANCE ────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// What the water shader needs from a boat is four numbers: where it is, which way it points, how
// fast it is going and how wide it is. This gathers them for anything afloat in the map window.
//
// ⚠ IT IS COLLECTED HERE AND NOT IN `drawYacht`, and that is an ORDERING fact rather than a
// preference. The GL water pass runs during the composite; the world sweep's ships are drawn after
// it, and a mark that queued itself through `emitFace` is not even resolved until the flush. A wake
// gathered there reaches the shader a frame late at best and never at worst — the trap the Curtain
// hit. The map, on the other hand, is sitting right here and already says where every ship is.
//
// ⚠ AND IT IS CAPPED, because the shader's array is. The nearest few are what a player can see
// doing anything; a harbour full of moored hulls at zero speed contributes nothing anyway, so the
// filter is on SPEED first and distance second.
function collectWakes(v, ax, ay, R) {
  if (!(RENDER_TUNE.glSwell > 0) || !v || !v.map) return null;
  const out = [];
  const push = (x, y, hdgDeg, spd, beam) => {
    if (!(spd > 0.02)) return;
    const r = (hdgDeg || 0) * Math.PI / 180;
    // ⚠ THE HEADING IS THE WORLD BEARING THE SHIP IS DRAWN AT, converted the same way `drawYacht`
    // converts it — north is -y here, which is the one sign in this file that is easiest to get
    // backwards and draws a wake coming out of the bow.
    out.push({ x, y, dx: Math.sin(r), dy: -Math.cos(r), spd: Math.min(1.4, spd), beam });
  };
  // Own ship, when she is one. The camera's ground point is where she is.
  //
  // ⚠ AND A BOAT GETS ONE WITHOUT BEING ASKED. `ownWake` was a finished slot with no producer
  // anywhere in the repo — the seam was built for a hull the sim did not have yet — and leaving it
  // as the only route means a boat whose panel forgets one field cuts a wake nobody can see. So an
  // explicit `ownWake` still wins (a caller that knows its own beam should say so), and a water
  // class that says nothing falls back to the speed it is already reporting. The beam is narrow
  // because this is a race hull rather than a yacht: 0.35 is the Echelon's.
  if (v.ownWake && v.ownWake.spd > 0.02) push(ax, ay, v.heading, v.ownWake.spd, v.ownWake.beam || 0.35);
  else if (!v.ownWake && v.cls && WATER_CLASSES().has(v.cls) && (v.speed || 0) > 0.02) {
    push(ax, ay, v.heading, v.speed, 0.20);
  }
  // And every ship the window is carrying. A cell's index maps to the window frame as (x - R).
  const N = R * 2 + 1;
  for (let y = 0; y < N && out.length < 6; y++) {
    const row = v.map[y];
    if (!row) continue;
    for (let x = 0; x < N && out.length < 6; x++) {
      const c = row[x];
      if (!c || c.mark !== 'yacht' || !c.wake) continue;
      push(x - R, y - R, c.heading ?? c.markHdg ?? 0, c.wake.spd || 0, 0.35);
    }
  }
  return out.length ? out : null;
}


// ── FOAM THAT STAYS WHERE THE WATER WAS DISTURBED ────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `seaWake` draws a bow crest, a trough and the Kelvin arms from a hull's CURRENT position, so the
// whole wake is rigidly attached to the boat: turn, and it swings round with you. What a real wake
// leaves behind is foam on the water where the hull has already been, which keeps going straight
// after the boat has turned away from it and takes a while to disperse.
//
// ⚠ IT IS THE SNOW TRACK STORE WITH A DIFFERENT DECAY LAW, and that is the whole implementation —
// see `client/shared/trail-store.js`. A rut is buried by later snowfall and a wake disperses on its
// own; everything else about laying a polyline behind a moving thing is identical, and all five of
// the rules that were hard to get right are stated once over there.
//
// ⚠ AND FOAM IS THE ONE PART OF THE SEA THAT NOTHING READS BACK, which is what makes it affordable
// where a spectral ocean is not. No physics samples it, no server verifies it, no gate re-derives
// it — so it is allowed to be per-client, stateful and approximate, and none of the determinism
// that the rest of this file is built around applies to it.
//
// ⚠ AND A WAKE IS ONE BAND, NEVER TWO RAILS. The snow shader measures `abs(d - half)` because a
// vehicle has two contact patches and leaves two marks with clean snow between them; a hull is one
// object and churns a single strip. Carrying the rail form across would draw a boat's wake as two
// parallel lines with untouched water down the middle, which is a catamaran.
const FOAM_MAX_PTS = 32;        // the uniform array, and therefore the whole budget
const FOAM_PER_SRC = 48;
const FOAM_SRC_MAX = 6;
const FOAM_STEP = 0.40;         // tiles before the live head is committed — tighter than a rut's
const FOAM_TURN = 0.12;
const FOAM_CREEP = 0.05;
const FOAM_JUMP = FOAM_STEP * 5;
// ⚠ HOW LONG FOAM LASTS IS THE FEATURE. Too short and it is the rigid wake again with extra steps;
// too long and a harbour fills up with white scribble that never clears. Forty seconds is about
// what a displacement hull's track holds in a moderate sea before it is indistinguishable from the
// chop around it.
const FOAM_AGE_MS = 40e3;
const FOAM_SPREAD = 2.6;        // how many times its own beam a trail has widened to by the end
const WAKES = new Map();        // id -> { pts: [{x, y, t, beam}], seen }
const foamFill = (q, now) => (now - q.t) / FOAM_AGE_MS;
const FOAM_BUF = new Float32Array(FOAM_MAX_PTS * 4);

// Called once per painted frame, beside the snow tracks.
// Exported as a TEST SEAM: foam is the one part of the sea with memory, so the only way to assert
// anything about it is to drive successive frames — which a gate can do and a single paint cannot.
// ⚠ A BENCH SEAM, and it exists because a stale trail silently poisons every measurement taken
// after it. The store is per-SESSION and ages on the wall clock, so a shot taken minutes after a
// sail still carries that boat's foam, decaying by a different amount between each pair of paints —
// which reads as a 4.5% noise floor on a scene with no boat in it, and buries any effect smaller
// than that. Nothing in the game needs this: there the world is continuous and the trail is the
// trail. A harness that paints unrelated scenes back to back does.
export function foamReset() { WAKES.clear(); }

export function foamFeed(v, now) {
  if (!(RENDER_TUNE.glFoam > 0) || !(RENDER_TUNE.glSwell > 0)) { if (WAKES.size) WAKES.clear(); return; }
  const wc = v && v.mapCenter; if (!wc) return;
  const R = v.map && v.map.length ? (v.map.length - 1) / 2 : 0;
  // ⚠ THE SAME GATHERER THE RIGID WAKE USES, so a hull that cuts a wake lays foam and one that does
  // not lays none, with no second opinion about what counts as a boat or how fast it has to be
  // going. It answers in WINDOW-relative tiles and a trail is stored in WORLD ones, because the
  // window recentres as you sail and a trail that moved with it would be the conveyor belt that
  // `seaScroll` was removed for.
  const hulls = collectWakes(v, (v.mapOffset && v.mapOffset.x) || 0, (v.mapOffset && v.mapOffset.y) || 0, R);
  if (!hulls) return;
  for (let i = 0; i < hulls.length; i++) {
    const h = hulls[i];
    // ⚠ THE OWN SHIP IS ALWAYS INDEX 0 WHEN SHE IS PRESENT, and every other hull is identified by
    // the TILE it was found on — which is the only handle `collectWakes` offers. A ship that crosses
    // a tile boundary therefore starts a new run rather than continuing one, and that is visible as
    // a break rather than as a wrong line: the alternative is guessing which of two hulls a moved
    // one used to be, and getting that wrong draws a gouge between two ships.
    const own = i === 0 && (v.ownWake || (v.cls && WATER_CLASSES().has(v.cls)));
    const wx = wc.x + h.x, wy = wc.y + h.y;
    const id = own ? '@own' : ('w:' + Math.round(wx) + ',' + Math.round(wy));
    layTrailPoint(WAKES, id, wx, wy, {
      now, fill: foamFill,
      stamp: () => ({ t: now, beam: h.beam }),
      step: FOAM_STEP, turn: FOAM_TURN, creep: FOAM_CREEP,
      perSrc: FOAM_PER_SRC, srcMax: FOAM_SRC_MAX,
    });
  }
}

export function foamUpload(v, now) {
  if (!(RENDER_TUNE.glFoam > 0) || !WAKES.size) return null;
  const wc = v.mapCenter; if (!wc) return null;
  const lim = (v.map && v.map.length ? (v.map.length - 1) / 2 : 24) + 6;
  const up = uploadTrail(WAKES, {
    buf: FOAM_BUF, maxPts: FOAM_MAX_PTS, cx: wc.x, cy: wc.y, lim, now,
    fill: foamFill, jump: FOAM_JUMP,
  });
  if (!up) return null;
  // The widest a point in this buffer will have spread to, which is what the bounding box has to
  // clear — a box drawn to the fresh beam clips the oldest and widest part of every trail.
  let beam = 0.2;
  for (const { src } of up.near) for (const q of src.pts) if (q.beam > beam) beam = q.beam;
  const pad = beam * FOAM_SPREAD + 0.1;
  return { pts: FOAM_BUF.subarray(0, up.n * 4), n: up.n, beam, spread: FOAM_SPREAD,
    box: [up.minX - pad, up.minY - pad, up.maxX + pad, up.maxY + pad] };
}

function seaPoseAt(wx, wy, now) {
  const a = seaAmps();
  if (!(a.roll > 0.0001 || a.wind > 0.0001)) return null;
  // ⚠ THE SHARED CLOCK, the same one the floor and the mesh run on. `now * 0.001` here would put
  // the hull on this tab's own timeline while the water it floats in is on everybody else's — and
  // a boat riding a sea nobody else can see is the exact failure the shared clock exists to remove.
  const t = now == null ? SEA_NOW.t : seaClock(now);
  const u = wx, v = wy;
  // ⚠ THE ROLL AND THE WIND SEA, AND NOT THE CHOP — exactly what the MESH displaces. The chop is
  // ~1 tile, shorter than a hull's beam, so a boat fitted to it is fitted to noise; and it is not
  // in the geometry anybody can see, so riding it would be riding a sea nobody is drawing.
  return { h: seaRoll(u, v, t, a.roll) + seaWind(u, v, t, a.wind) };
}
// THE OWN SHIP SHADOW CANNOT REACH DECAL_SINK DIRECTLY, and the reason is ordering rather than
// taste: it is painted outside the worldBlend block (a parked craft reads as planted the instant
// you embark) and therefore BEFORE drawWorldObjects opens the sinks. Filling a sink that is still
// null is the flush-timing trap the Curtain and the scatter each hit once. So it is stashed here
// and pushed when the sink opens — one frame, one variable, cleared in the same finally as the
// rest.
let OWN_SHADOWS = null;

// ── THE ROOM YOU ARE SITTING IN ──────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `client/shared/interior-shell.js` holds the shape and the reasons; this is the twenty lines that
// put it in the world. A shell is authored in metres about the driver's eye, so all this does is
// scale it by the seat's own eye height, turn it onto the VEHICLE's heading, and drop it at the
// camera's tile.
//
// ⚠ THE VEHICLE'S HEADING, NEVER THE CAMERA'S. By the time this runs `v.heading` is the EYE's —
// a shoulder-check, a peek and an orbit have all been added to it — and a cab that turned with
// your head would be a cab you can never look away from: the pillar would sit in front of your
// nose at every angle and the world would slide past behind a frame that never moved. `ownHdg`
// is the rig's own, and it is carried on the view for exactly this class of question.
//
// ⚠ AND THE LEAN IS LEFT OUT OF THE ANCHOR ON PURPOSE. `cam.fx`/`cam.fy` move the EYE inside the
// cab (that is what a lean is — a translation, and the only thing that moves near objects more
// than far ones). Anchoring the shell at the leaned eye would carry the room along with the head
// and cancel the whole effect: you would lean and nothing would move. The shell is pinned to the
// unleaned eye and `cam.proj` applies the lean to it exactly as it does to a building.
//
// ⚠ IT IS A SOLID AND IT IS CLOSE, so it is in OWNSHIP_SINK rather than in the mass: the mass
// buffer is cached per map window and this moves every frame. That also gets it into the PUDDLE
// for free — the reflection pass draws the solids, and a cab that was missing from its own
// reflection is the bug gl/solids.js was built to fix, one object further in.
function pushInteriorShell(cam, v) {
  if (!OWNSHIP_SINK || !RENDER_TUNE.interior || v.external || v.bare) return 0;
  const P = shellProfileFor(v.cls);
  if (!P) return 0;
  // ⚠ THE LIVE VALUES GO IN HERE AND NOWHERE ELSE. A boat's dials are geometry (see the
  // instruments block in interior-shell.js), so the needle's angle is a VERTEX POSITION and has to
  // be known at build time. `shellFaces` is called fresh every frame and is pure in its two
  // arguments, so this costs a rebuild of about a hundred small quads and buys a cluster that
  // turns with your head, sits in the depth buffer and appears in the boat's own reflection.
  const faces = shellFaces(P, v.instr || null);
  if (!faces.length) return 0;

  // Metres to tiles, through the seat's own eye height — see `eyeMetresOf`, which is per profile
  // and falls back to `EYE_M`.
  const S = (cam.EH || 0.12) / eyeMetresOf(P);
  const hd = ((v.ownHdg != null ? v.ownHdg : v.heading) || 0) * Math.PI / 180;
  const sh = Math.sin(hd), ch = Math.cos(hd);
  // Local (right, forward) onto the map window, using the same two basis vectors `makeCam` reads
  // the world through — forward is (sinh, -cosh) and right is (cosh, sinh), so a shell built here
  // and a building projected there cannot disagree about which way the truck is pointing.
  const ox = cam.ox || 0, oy = cam.oy || 0, ez = cam.EH;

  // The light, and the relation `drawCabInterior` shades every surface by: what comes in through
  // the screen, in the sky's own colour, floored so a pillar return keeps its thickness at night.
  const T = v.cls === 'truck' ? cabTrim(v.tier, v.trim) : DASH_COLOURWAYS.slate;
  const sky = skyAt(v.hour == null ? 12 : v.hour);
  const murk = v.weather === 'storm' || v.weather === 'ash' || v.weather === 'dust' ? 0.55
    : v.weather === 'rain' || v.weather === 'snow' || v.weather === 'fog' || v.weather === 'haze' ? 0.40
    : v.weather === 'overcast' ? 0.22 : 0;
  const litK = clamp(Math.max(sky.night, murk), 0, 1);
  const KEY = mix([10, 12, 16], sky.hor, 0.88);
  const outK = clamp(1 - litK * 0.88, 0.12, 1);
  // Every tone is a key the colourway already carries — see the ⚠ in interior-shell.js. `seat` and
  // `floor` are the two surfaces a dashboard has no word for, so they are derived off the ones it
  // does rather than authored: a seat is the pillar trim in cloth and a floor is the darkest thing
  // in the cab, which is what a rubber mat under a driver's boots actually is.
  const baseOf = (tone) => {
    const pick = tone === 'floor' ? (T.dash && T.dash[2]) : tone === 'seat' ? (T.pil && T.pil[0])
      : tone === 'dash' ? (T.dash && T.dash[1]) : tone === 'hdr' ? (T.hdr && T.hdr[0])
      : tone === 'pil' ? (T.pil && T.pil[1]) : T.post;
    return hex2rgb(pick) || [40, 44, 50];
  };

  let n = 0;
  for (const f of faces) {
    // The forward aperture, for as long as the painted one is up — see the ⚠ in interior-shell.js.
    if (f.fwd && CAB_PAINTED) continue;
    const c = baseOf(f.tone);
    const k = f.k;
    // The cab relation, with ONE deliberate difference on the dark side, and it is a difference of
    // SUBJECT rather than of taste. In the 2-D cab every negative-k surface is an EDGE — a pillar
    // return, a dash roll, a corner moulding — read against a bright windscreen a few pixels away,
    // where going almost black is what makes the edge an edge. Here they are WHOLE WALLS that fill
    // the frame when you turn round, and at 0.80 the back of the cab arrives as a featureless black
    // rectangle: correct about the light, and indistinguishable from the renderer having failed.
    // 0.55 keeps the same ordering — the lining is still darker than the door, the door than the
    // dash — and leaves enough separation to read the shape by.
    // ⚠ AN INSTRUMENT STATES ITS OWN COLOUR AND MAY REFUSE THE LIGHT. Everything else in here is a
    // key off the colourway shaded by what comes in through the screen, which is right for a panel
    // and wrong for a lamp: a lit segment shaded by the ambient goes out at dusk, which is exactly
    // when a dial has to be readable. `emis` is how far the face ignores the cabin light — 0 is an
    // ordinary surface and 1 is a source — so an unlit segment still darkens with the rest of the
    // board and a lit one does not.
    const base = f.rgb || c;
    const shaded = k >= 0 ? mix(base, KEY, k * outK * 0.62) : mix(base, [3, 4, 6], -k * 0.55);
    const lit = f.emis ? mix(shaded, base, clamp(f.emis, 0, 1)) : shaded;
    OWNSHIP_SINK.push({
      p: f.p.map((q) => {
        const rx = q[0] * S, fy = q[1] * S;
        return [rx * ch + fy * sh + ox, rx * sh - fy * ch + oy, ez + q[2] * S];
      }),
      // ⚠ THE TAG IS FOR THE CENSUSES, NOT FOR THE RENDERER — gl/solids.js reads `p`, `rgb` and `a`
      // and ignores everything else. OWNSHIP_SINK had one producer when `ownship.mjs` and
      // `yacht.mjs` were written, so both of them count the whole buffer and call the answer the
      // rig; with a second producer in it they reported 72 faces of a cab as a truck collected from
      // inside its own seat, and as a yacht hull that changes size between two headings. Same
      // answer the bird faces already carry for the same reason: a check that has to INFER which
      // object a face belongs to from where it sits in the buffer breaks the first time anything
      // else joins it.
      rgb: [lit[0] | 0, lit[1] | 0, lit[2] | 0], a: 1, interior: 1,
    });
    n++;
  }
  INTERIOR_TALLY = { faces: n, cls: v.cls, eyeH: cam.EH, scale: S };
  return n;
}
// What the last frame's shell put in the buffer. A census rather than a log: the gate and
// __glass2() both need to tell 'the shell drew nothing' from 'the shell is switched off', and
// those are the same picture from outside.
let INTERIOR_TALLY = null;
export function interiorTally() { return INTERIOR_TALLY; }
if (typeof window !== 'undefined') window.__interior = () => INTERIOR_TALLY;
// AND THE CONTACTS, FOR THE MIRROR-IMAGE REASON. The shadow is painted too EARLY (before the
// sinks open) and a contact is painted too LATE — drawContacts runs after drawWorldObjects has
// composited and cleared everything. So the models are baked in a pass of their own before the
// world pass and wait here for a sink to exist.
let LATE_BILLBOARDS = null;
// ── ⚠ AND EVERYTHING THE AIRFIELD STANDS ON ITS GROUND, FOR THE SHADOW'S REASON EXACTLY ─────
//
// `drawGroundSurfaces` runs BEFORE drawWorldObjects, and drawWorldObjects is where SPRITE_SINK,
// STROKE_SINK and DECAL_SINK are armed — so for the whole of the ground pass all three are null
// and anything it draws takes the canvas path. That was survivable while the ground pass only
// painted FLAT tarmac, which is what GROUND_MESH is for. It is not survivable for the things
// STANDING on that tarmac: a windsock, a runway edge light, a PAPI lamp, a helipad's TLOF ring.
// Those paint onto the 2-D canvas and then `drawWorldObjects` blits the GL canvas over the whole
// ground plane — opaque, since GROUND_FULL — and wipes every one of them.
//
// ⚠ MEASURED RATHER THAN REASONED: in a GLASS 2 frame over Coldwater Regional, 72 of 72 runway
// edge-light ops and 4 of 4 windsock fabric ops were issued BEFORE that blit. The airport had no
// lights at night and the socks were gone, which is exactly how it was reported.
//
// So the airfield dressing is DEFERRED rather than moved: the ground pass pushes a closure here
// and drawWorldObjects runs it the moment the sinks exist, beside the shadow's own drain. The
// drawers need to know nothing about any of this — they run with the sinks open and route
// themselves. ⚠ With no GL pass this is null and every one of them runs INLINE, at the point in
// the ground pass it always ran at, so the 2-D picture is the one that always shipped.
// ⚠ AND THE CLOSURE MUST CAPTURE ITS ALPHA AS A VALUE. The ground pass sets ctx.globalAlpha per
// tile for the far fade and restores it when it returns; by the time these run it is whatever the
// world pass has. Every one of them takes an explicit `alpha` argument for that reason.
let GROUND_LATE = null;
// How many droplets the last frame put in the air. Reported because spray is otherwise unobservable
// headlessly — every harness here installs a GL hook that answers null and reaches no draw call.
let SPRAY_TALLY = 0;
export function sprayTally() { return SPRAY_TALLY; }
// Run it now, or once there is a depth buffer to run it against.
function emitGroundLate(fn) { if (GROUND_LATE) GROUND_LATE.push(fn); else fn(); }
// What drawMode7Floor would have rastered, as uniforms plus two LUT planes. See gl/floor.js.
let FLOOR_STATE = null;
// ── THE GROUND'S OWN STATE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `wet` is the FILM (0-1), `pond` the water STANDING in it, `snow` how deep the snow lies, and
// `fell` everything that has fallen — monotonic, and the only one of the four that keeps rising
// for as long as it is snowing, which is what a wheel rut needs (see TRACK_BURY).
//
// ⚠ ONE OBJECT RATHER THAN FOUR SCALARS, because the arithmetic is shared with the server now and
// the shared step has to be handed something it can write to. The time constants moved with it —
// `POND_RISE_S`/`POND_DRAIN_S`/`SNOW_LIE_S`/`SNOW_THAW_S` went with it and still mean what they
// meant — the ⚠ beside each of them is in client/shared/ground-accum.js, and so is every reader,
// which is why this file no longer imports one.
//
// ⚠ AND IT IS SEEDED FROM THE SERVER ON THE FIRST FRAME THAT CAN BE — see `GROUND_SEEDED`. Module
// scope rather than per-view: the weather is the weather, and two seats painting one frame should
// not disagree about whether it has been raining.
const GROUND = freshGround();
let GROUND_SEEDED = false;
// How much the air near the ground hazes, off WX_HAZE — see the integration above and gl/fog.js.
let FOG_H_NOW = 0.12;
// ⚠ AND `wetForce` IS THE BENCH'S WAY IN, because there is no other one. The wetness is integrated
// from live precipitation the SERVER sends, so a Modelshop scene — which has no weather field at
// all — sits at 0 for ever and every measurement of the wet road comes back as a measurement of a
// dry one. That is the same shape as the four confounded attempts already recorded against Phase 6:
// a correctly wired feature and a harness with no way to switch it on look identical. null means
// follow the weather, which is what the game always does.
const wetGround = () => (RENDER_TUNE.glWet || 0) * (RENDER_TUNE.wetForce == null ? GROUND.wet : RENDER_TUNE.wetForce);
// The street-level fog density, per tile. `fogHForce` pins the haze the way `wetForce` pins the
// water — a bench seam, and the only way to stand in fog without waiting for the weather to bring
// some. The tune folds in here so 0 is an exact off switch rather than a small number.
const fogGround = () => (RENDER_TUNE.glFogH || 0) * (RENDER_TUNE.fogHForce == null ? FOG_H_NOW : RENDER_TUNE.fogHForce);
// How hard that air scatters a light back at you.
//
// ⚠ THE FLOOR IS THE POINT, AND IT SITS JUST ABOVE `clear`. WX_HAZE puts clear at 0.12, so a floor
// at 0.18 makes an ordinary night exactly 0 — the shader skips the whole per-fragment loop on a
