    // ⚠ NO OUTLINE OVER A DEPTH-BUFFERED MODEL. The stroke follows a face's WHOLE outline, including
    // the part of it that lost the depth test, so it would draw the hidden half of every box as a
    // wireframe over the panel standing in front of it. Half its job was hiding sort seams anyway,
    // and there are none left to hide.
    //
    // ⚠ AND `bodyOnGL` IS THE OTHER HALF OF THAT SENTENCE, WHICH THE FILL GOT AND THIS DID NOT.
    // A contact whose triangles went to gl/solids.js comes back through here for the four detail
    // passes, and the two escape hatches this line carries — `!rasterOK` and `offBuf` — are both
    // about the model's OWN software depth pass, which such a contact no longer uses. `!rasterOK`
    // is the common one: the raster declines any model under `RASTER_MIN_PX`, so every vehicle in
    // traffic small enough to skip it took this branch with its fill suppressed four lines up and
    // its OUTLINE drawn anyway — on the canvas, which is composited after the whole city. That is a
    // wireframe of a lorry hanging through the building it is driving behind, and it is the one
    // thing on screen the depth buffer had already got right.
    //
