// ── THE SECOND BATCH: THE ARMS THAT SERVE MORE THAN ONE BUILDING ────────────
//
// Every table below was authored against `shapeForModel`'s own numbers for that arm rather than
// against a reading of its code, which is what stops a coping band floating over its building.
//
// ⚠ AND `half`/`cy` IS THE BOX'S OWN FOOTPRINT MULTIPLIER, CAPPED AT 1.0. `draw3DBoxAt` clamps a
// half-width to 0.44, so a box authored at `fh * 1.15` genuinely has its wall at 1.1 fh-units by
// the time fh reaches 0.4 — author the part at 1.15 and it hangs off the side. Author UNDER the
// clamp and it is at worst a few centimetres inside a wall, which nothing can see.
//
// ⚠ AND NONE OF THEM CARRIES A `signBoard`. Thirteen of these fifteen arms already sign themselves
// with a `neonBlade` or a `marqueeBand`, so a board would be a second sign on the same frontage —
// and the two that do not (a boarded-up unit, a wharf shed) are buildings that should not be
// signed at all. Signage is an arm's business; trim is this table's.

// A shut-up shop under a lived-in block: a slab to 0.94h at fh*0.86, a band across it to 1.00h at
// fh*0.92, and the shopfront panel to 0.30h — one storey — on the front face. That band is the ONLY
// roof-bearing box in the arm, so it is the only place coping can go; the rest is the service run
// nobody came back for.
//
// ⚠ EVERY z HERE MOVED WITH THE WALL, AND A PART THAT DID NOT WOULD BE SILENT ABOUT IT. These were
// authored against a 0.78h roofline: the parapet at 0.88 is now a coping band floating a sixth of a
// building over its own roof, and the riser run at 0.66–0.72 is a conduit stopping two thirds of
// the way up a wall for no reason. `anchored.mjs` is the gate for the first and nothing catches the
// second — it just reads as a building somebody stopped detailing.
//
// ⚠ AND THE SHOPFRONT IS A `shutter` SO THE KIT CAN SEE IT. It is MASS in the arm, and the name
// solver's own ⚠ says `blocks` is built from the DETAIL list and cannot see mass — which is exactly
// how a shopfront slab a tenth of a tile proud of its wall ends up with the building's name behind
// it. Declaring it here does two things at once: it puts the slab in `blocks`, so the name is
// lifted clear of it, and it claims the `ground` section, so the kit stops dressing a frontage the
// arm has already drawn — an empty unit whose windows the kit lights is the one thing this model
// may not be.
//
// ⚠ AND THE LAST THREE ENTRIES EACH CLAIM A SECTION OFF THE KIT, WHICH IS THE POINT OF THEM. A
// `fireEscape` takes `stair` and a `stack` takes `roof`, so the nine balconies and the water tank,
// bulkhead and four condensers the kit was standing up there all stop. That is the right trade for
// this building and only for this building: what a slum walk-up has on its flank is an iron stair,
// and what it has on its roof is chimneys — the kit's roof is a commercial block's, and its nine
// balconies are a better building's. The `wall` section is deliberately NOT claimed: the kit's
// twelve front bays are seeded, lit per room and better than a static table could be.
const D_VACANT = [
  { kind: 'parapet', z: [0, 1.00, 0], half: [0.92, 0, 0], hh: [0, 0.022, 0] },
  // The iron stair down one flank — a walk-up over a shop has no second way out through the shop.
  // ⚠ `face: 'x'` IS WHAT PUTS IT ON A FLANK and the sign of `cy` picks which; see the ⚠ on `Eside`
  // in detailLayer. It starts at first-floor level, because a fire escape that reached the pavement
  // would be a ladder anybody could climb, which is the reason real ones stop where they do.
  { kind: 'fireEscape', face: 'x', cx: [0.18, 0, 0], cy: [-0.86, 0, 0], z0: [0, 0.34, 0], z1: [0, 0.94, 0], half: [0.25, 0, 0], out: [0.12, 0, 0], flights: 3 },
  // Two chimney stacks and the aerials beside them. ⚠ STANDING KINDS RISE FROM `z`, so this is the
  // deck they stand on — the parapet band's own top at 1.00h — rather than their middle.
  { kind: 'stack', cx: [-0.36, 0, 0], cy: [-0.22, 0, 0], z: [0, 1.00, 0], r: [0.050, 0, 0], hh: [0, 0.105, 0], pal: 'ty_unit_brick' },
  { kind: 'stack', cx: [0.34, 0, 0], cy: [-0.26, 0, 0], z: [0, 1.00, 0], r: [0.044, 0, 0], hh: [0, 0.082, 0], pal: 'ty_unit_brick' },
  { kind: 'antennaCluster', cx: [0.02, 0, 0], cy: [0.24, 0, 0], z: [0, 1.00, 0], r: [0.11, 0, 0], hh: [0, 0.085, 0] },
  // ⚠ `cy` 0.90 AND NOT 0.88, WHICH IS THE ARM'S REVEAL, AND NOT 0.86, WHICH IS THE WALL. The
  // frontage is four surfaces in one place — wall, reveal, closure, then whatever the state hangs
  // on it — and on a painter they sort by `DETAIL_LIFT` while on a depth buffer they COMPARE. Two
  // of them at one plane is a tie, and a tie is a z-fight rather than a missing part, so it is the
  // one defect here that would look like a driver bug. The ladder is stated in the arm.
  // ⚠ AND `pal` IS EXPLICIT: without it the painter shades the curtain off `c.pal`, which is the
  // WALL, so the shut door is two shades of the brick it is set into.
  // ⚠ AND IT IS OFF CENTRE AND NARROWER THAN THE FRONTAGE, because the frontage is not all shop.
  // A walk-up over a shop has its own street door beside the shop's, and the arm draws that; the
  // curtain has to leave room for it or the two overlap and the door reads as a patch on the
  // shutter. The two numbers are stated together in the arm, off `SHOPW`.
  { kind: 'shutter', cx: [-0.13, 0, 0], cy: [0.90, 0, 0], z: [0, 0.15, 0], half: [0.48, 0, 0], hh: [0, 0.15, 0], pal: 'ty_unit_shut' },
  { kind: 'conduit', cy: [0.86, 0, 0], z: [0, 0.80, 0], half: [0.66, 0, 0], r: [0.012, 0, 0] },
  { kind: 'cableRun', cy: [0.86, 0, 0], z: [0, 0.86, 0], half: [0.78, 0, 0], sag: [0, 0.055, 0], r: [0.009, 0, 0] },
  { kind: 'vent', cx: [0.72, 0, 0], cy: [0.86, 0, 0], z: [0, 0.44, 0], w: [0.10, 0, 0], hh: [0, 0.045, 0] },
];

